  "copy": {
    "fr": {
      "tagline": "Ton ami marshmallow saute de rocher en rocher. Grimpe sans t'arrêter, sinon tu brûles.",
      "tags": [
        "Tap pour sauter",
        "Sans fin",
        "Lave"
      ]
    },
    "en": {
      "tagline": "Your marshmallow friend hops from rock to rock. Keep climbing or you burn.",
      "tags": [
        "Tap to jump",
        "Endless",
        "Lava"
      ]
    }
  },
  "description": "A marshmallow hangs over a lava lake, stuck to the only thing keeping it up: a falling rock. A tap flings it along the line to your finger at one fixed speed — no drag, no charge — and it sticks to the first thing it touches, then rides that rock down. Rocks melt when they reach the lava, so a landing is never a rest: the only way to stay alive is to keep climbing the ones still falling. Land high and the score pays more, chain fresh rocks and the multiplier grows. Fireballs can be grabbed too, but they only lend their back for a beat before shrugging the body off — one shade browner if you stayed. Holding the finger slows the world, but only while the focus meter under it lasts: it drains as you deliberate and the slow motion fades with it, so a long aim buys less time and a shorter preview. It stays locked for a beat once you let go before it starts filling again, and landing on a fresh rock hands part of it straight back. The lava rises the whole run, and it is the only clock. The toast is the life — white, golden, toasted, charred — and a hit on a charred marshmallow ends the run: staying on a fireball until it throws you off, or the serpent's bite. The lava is three hits at once, so only a white marshmallow comes out of it, charred. On the web the climb runs through five biomes of six levels, each bringing one thing that changes the read: the caldera and its fireballs, frozen sulphur whose ice throws the body straight up, geysers whose steam lifts whatever it touches, fault lines of rocks that split into pieces, fall to dust or blow two seconds after a landing, and the core, where the lava surges and drips. Further up the climb, giant lava bubbles carry the body up before they pop, cold water sets crusts on the lake and puts fires out, magnet crystals bend a jump, ash gusts push it, a lava serpent leaps out of a swirl, and s'mores drift down: a biscuit and a chocolate give a life back, a whole super s'more gives them all back.",
